  "question": {
    "text": "A horizontal uniform glass tube of <math> <mrow> <mtext>100</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow> </math> length, sealed at both ends, contains <math> <mrow> <mtext>10</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow> </math> mercury column in the middle. The temperature and pressure of air on either side of mercury column are respectively <math> <mrow> <mn>31</mn><mmultiscripts> <mi>C</mi> <mprescripts></mprescripts> <none></none> <mo>∘</mo> </mmultiscripts> </mrow> </math> and <math> <mrow> <mn>76</mn></mrow> </math> cm of mercury. If the air column at one end is kept at <math> <mrow> <mn>0</mn><mmultiscripts> <mi>C</mi> <mprescripts></mprescripts> <none></none> <mo>∘</mo> </mmultiscripts> </mrow> </math> and the other end at <math> <mrow> <mn>273</mn><mmultiscripts> <mi>C</mi> <mprescripts></mprescripts> <none></none> <mo>∘</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></math>, the pressure of air which is at <math> <mrow> <mn>0</mn><mmultiscripts> <mi>C</mi> <mprescripts></mprescripts> <none></none> <mo>∘</mo> </mmultiscripts> </mrow> </math> is (in <math> <mrow> <mtext>cm</mtext></mrow> </math> of <math> <mrow> <mtext>Hg</mtext></mrow></math>). Assure no heat flow through mercury"
